22) A banking application processes transactions. If an error occurs, such as
insufficient funds or invalid account number, the system should handle it
gracefully instead of crashing. Question: Define Exception Handling and Error
Handling and explain how they help in building reliable banking applications.
=== Exception Handling:
o In Java, exceptions are used to handle situations that might occur during the
execution of a program. Exceptions are conditions that a program can
anticipate and recover from.
o They represent runtime issues like invalid input, network errors, database
issues, etc.
o Try-catch blocks are used to handle exceptions.
o Example: Insufficient funds or invalid account numbers in a banking
application can throw exceptions that can be caught and handled to prevent
application crashes.
• Error Handling:
o Errors represent serious problems that a program cannot typically recover
from (e.g., out of memory, JVM errors, etc.).
o Errors are usually handled by the runtime environment rather than the
program itself.
o In general, errors are not caught by regular exception handling (using try-
catch blocks).
o Example: An OutOfMemoryError would indicate a serious issue that needs to
be addressed by the system or environment, not by handling it in code.
How They Help in Banking Applications:
• Exception Handling: Ensures that the application remains robust and doesn't crash
when common issues (like insufficient funds) occur. For example, the system could
prompt the user to check their balance when an insufficient funds exception is
caught.
• Error Handling: Helps to identify and log critical errors such as database or server
crashes that might require system-level intervention.
